; The nint kind of the lexeme-derived variant-name refusal pinned in
; choice_float_arm_variant_name.cddl: a fixed NINT arm mints its variant name from the value's
; lexeme (`-1` -> `U-1`, the `U`-for-nint prefix being existing naming behaviour), and the `-` is
; not spellable in a Rust identifier — so the arm rejects gracefully at parse rather than emitting
; a name that dies at rustfmt. Enumerated separately from the float kind because the two take
; different `FixedValue::for_variant` arms, and a kind proven on one certifies nothing about the
; other.
;   - `a`  bare rule-level type choice.
;   - `b`  bare-member GROUP choice — the second naming consumer.
; Green neighbours: `c` takes the supported `@name` route, and `d` is a fixed UINT arm, whose
; minted name (`I1`) IS an identifier — the refusal keys on the minted string, not on numeric-ness.
a = -1 / null / tstr
b = [ true // -1 ]
c = -1 ; @name neg_one
  / null / tstr
d = 1 / null / tstr
